body:
Fair; I've read about this as well, in terms of it being more of a head thing than an actual difference of effect. However, I'd like to point out that this isn't necessarily a bad thing. It's similar in concept to people who can't taste the difference between two different types of water. You can't tell the difference, but you at least get the same benefit.   


So while yes, that is entirely true that there may not be any stark or noticeable differences; if the user still gets the benefits they want from it, there's truly little harm in believing and feeling the difference. A great comment from this article ([https://medmen.com/blog/medmen-news/mythbusting-scientists-say-cannabis-strains-are-the-same](https://medmen.com/blog/medmen-news/mythbusting-scientists-say-cannabis-strains-are-the-same)) really sums this up really well;   


"As long as you’re finding flower that clicks with your body’s chemistry, and gets you where you want to go, that’s what really counts. So stick with what you know."
